COUNTRIES = {
    "belgium": {"name":"Belgium","code":"belgium","iso":"BE","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","deposit":"10-20%","ltv":"up to 90% for first-time buyers","govFirst":"Flanders offers a reduced 3% registration duty for primary residences (down from 6%). Brussels provides an abatement on the first €200,000 for properties up to €600,000. These regional incentives can save first-time buyers €10,000-20,000","savingsTips":"Open a spaarrekening (savings account) with a Belgian bank. Some banks offer preferential rates for housing savings. Consider reducing existing debts to improve your borrowing profile, as Belgian lenders look closely at your debt-to-income ratio","preApproval":"Get a credit simulation from multiple banks. BNP Paribas Fortis, KBC, ING, and Belfius all offer online simulators. A mortgage broker (hypotheekmakelaar) can compare across lenders for you","mistakes":"Not understanding the difference between registration duties across regions (6% Flanders vs 12.5% Wallonia/Brussels), underestimating notary fees (which include registration tax), forgetting schuldsaldoverzekering (outstanding balance insurance) costs, and skipping the building inspection because it is not legally required","costs_breakdown":"Registration duties are the largest cost: 3% in Flanders for primary residences, 12.5% in Wallonia and Brussels-Capital. Notary fees of 1-2% cover the deed transfer, title searches, and administrative costs. Mortgage registration tax is approximately 1% of the loan amount. Additional costs include building inspection (€300-500), energy performance certificate review, and moving expenses. Budget 5-8% in Flanders or 14-17% in Wallonia/Brussels on top of the purchase price","costs_hidden":"The schuldsaldoverzekering (outstanding balance insurance) is an often-overlooked cost that can run €2,000-10,000+ depending on age and health. Syndic fees for apartment buildings, annual property tax (onroerende voorheffing/précompte immobilier), and renovation costs for older Belgian properties can be substantial","mortgage_types":"Belgium offers three main mortgage types. **Vaste rentevoet (fixed rate)** locks your rate for the full term — this is the most popular choice, providing complete payment certainty. Monthly payments stay identical from first to last. **Variabele rentevoet (variable rate)** adjusts periodically (annually, every 3 years, or every 5 years) based on market conditions. Belgian law provides important protection: your variable rate can never more than double from its initial level, creating a legal safety net. **Semi-vaste rentevoet (semi-fixed)** offers a fixed rate for an initial period (typically 5-10 years) before converting to variable. This gives initial certainty with the possibility of benefiting from rate decreases later","mortgage_compare":"Fixed rate offers maximum certainty — your payment never changes. Variable rate starts lower but carries risk of increases (capped by law at double the initial rate). Semi-fixed gives a middle ground. In low-rate environments, locking in a fixed rate is generally recommended. In high-rate environments, variable may save money if rates are expected to decline","mortgage_tip":"Belgian variable rate mortgages have a unique legal protection — the rate can never more than double from the initial level. This cap makes variable rates less risky in Belgium than in most countries. Always compare the AEIR (Annual Effective Interest Rate) which includes all costs, not just the headline rate"},
    "brazil": {"name":"Brazil","code":"brazil","iso":"BR","sym":"R$","cb":"Central Bank of Brazil","deposit":"20-30%","ltv":"up to 80% through Caixa for FGTS-eligible buyers","govFirst":"Minha Casa Minha Vida provides subsidised interest rates as low as 4-5% for families earning up to R$8,000/month (compared to market rates of 9-12%). FGTS (Workers' Severance Fund) can be used for the deposit or to reduce monthly payments. First-time buyers with 3+ years of FGTS contributions get the best terms","savingsTips":"Build your FGTS balance by maintaining formal employment — employers deposit 8% of your salary. You can use FGTS every 2 years to reduce your mortgage. Open a poupança (savings account) for additional savings. Check your FGTS statement at caixa.gov.br","preApproval":"Check your eligibility with Caixa Econômica Federal first for FGTS-subsidised rates. Bring your CPF, income proof, and FGTS statement. Also check your credit score at Serasa Experian — scores above 700 get better terms","mistakes":"Not checking Caixa/FGTS eligibility before going to commercial banks (missing subsidised rates), underestimating ITBI transfer tax and cartório fees, choosing the PRICE amortisation system over SAC (which costs more overall), and not using FGTS balance to reduce the mortgage every 2 years","costs_breakdown":"ITBI (property transfer tax) of 2-3% depending on the municipality — in São Paulo it is 3%. Cartório fees for registry and deed of 1-2% of the property value. Real estate broker commission of 5-6% is typically paid by the seller. Bank processing fee (tarifa de avaliação) of R$2,000-3,000. Mandatory insurance: MIP (death/disability) and DFI (property damage) are added to monthly payments. Moving costs and any renovation expenses. Total additional costs: 4-6% of the purchase price","costs_hidden":"Mandatory insurance (MIP and DFI) adds significantly to your monthly EMI — often 15-25% on top of the principal and interest payment. IPTU (annual property tax) varies widely by municipality. Condominium fees (taxa de condomínio) for apartments can be R$500-2,000/month. Building maintenance fund (fundo de reserva) contributions are common","mortgage_types":"Brazil has two main amortisation systems that fundamentally affect your total cost. **SAC (Sistema de Amortização Constante)** keeps principal payments constant — monthly payments start higher but decrease over time as interest on the reducing balance falls. Total cost is lower. **PRICE (Tabela Price)** keeps total monthly payments constant — easier to budget but you pay more total interest. Beyond amortisation, rates are linked to either the **TR (Taxa Referencial)** — a government-set reference rate that has been near zero — or **IPCA (inflation index)** — which adjusts with inflation, adding uncertainty. FGTS-linked loans through Caixa offer the lowest rates","mortgage_compare":"SAC starts with higher payments but costs significantly less over the full term — often saving R$50,000-150,000 on a 20-year loan. PRICE has consistent payments that are easier to budget. TR-linked loans have been favourable as TR has remained near zero, while IPCA-linked loans carry inflation risk. Always choose SAC if you can afford the higher initial payments — the savings are substantial","mortgage_tip":"Always check Caixa first — their FGTS-linked rates can be 3-5% lower than commercial bank rates. Use FGTS every 2 years to reduce your outstanding balance. If rates drop, use portabilidade (mortgage portability) to switch banks for free — the original bank has 5 days to match the competing offer"},
    "canada": {"name":"Canada","code":"canada","iso":"CA","sym":"$","cb":"Bank of Canada","deposit":"5-20%","ltv":"up to 95% with CMHC insurance","govFirst":"The First Home Savings Account (FHSA) allows tax-deductible contributions up to $8,000/year ($40,000 lifetime). The Home Buyers' Plan (HBP) lets you withdraw up to $60,000 from your RRSP tax-free. First-time buyers receive land transfer tax rebates in Ontario (up to $4,000) and BC. These programmes can provide $30,000-100,000+ toward your first home","savingsTips":"Open an FHSA immediately — contributions are tax-deductible and grow tax-free, combining the best features of an RRSP and TFSA. Max out the FHSA ($8,000/year) before contributing to the HBP. Use a TFSA for additional savings. Set up automatic transfers on payday","preApproval":"Get pre-approved through a mortgage broker — they access dozens of lenders and their service is free to you (paid by the lender). Pre-approval locks in a rate for 90-120 days. You must pass the stress test at the higher of your contract rate + 2% or the qualifying rate","mistakes":"Waiving the home inspection to win a bidding war (risky and increasingly discouraged), not understanding the stress test (which reduces your maximum borrowing by 15-20%), forgetting to budget for CMHC insurance on high-ratio mortgages (2.8-4% of the mortgage), and not comparing rates across lenders at renewal","costs_breakdown":"Land transfer tax varies by province: Ontario charges 0.5-2.5% on a sliding scale (Toronto adds a municipal LTT doubling the cost), BC charges 1-5%, Alberta has no LTT. CMHC/Sagen/Canada Guaranty mortgage insurance of 2.8-4% of the mortgage amount for deposits under 20%. Legal fees of $1,500-2,500. Home inspection of $300-500. Title insurance of $300-500. Property tax adjustment on closing. Moving costs. Total additional costs: 3-5% (up to 8% in Toronto with municipal LTT)","costs_hidden":"CMHC insurance is the biggest surprise for many first-time buyers — on a $500,000 home with 5% down, the premium is approximately $19,000, added to the mortgage. In Toronto, the double land transfer tax (provincial + municipal) can add $12,000-15,000 on a $600,000 purchase. Condo maintenance fees of $400-800/month are an ongoing cost that lenders factor into affordability","mortgage_types":"Canadian mortgages are unique because the **term** (when your rate expires) is much shorter than the **amortisation** (when the loan is fully paid). A **fixed-rate mortgage** locks in your rate for the term (typically 5 years) — the most popular choice, providing payment certainty. A **variable-rate mortgage** fluctuates with the prime rate — lower initial cost but payments change when the Bank of Canada moves rates. An **adjustable-rate mortgage (ARM)** adjusts your payment amount with prime, while variable-rate may keep payments steady but adjust the principal/interest split. At term renewal (every 1-5 years), you renegotiate your rate — unlike the US 30-year fixed, Canadian rates change regularly","mortgage_compare":"The 5-year fixed is Canada's most popular product — reliable payments and no surprises. Variable/adjustable rates have historically been cheaper over time but carry risk when rates rise sharply (as seen in 2022-2023). Shorter terms (1-3 year fixed) offer lower rates but more frequent renewals. The key Canadian insight: your rate changes every 1-5 years regardless, so the long-term difference between fixed and variable is smaller than in countries with 25-30 year fixed rates","mortgage_tip":"Use a mortgage broker for your first purchase — they compare dozens of lenders for free. At renewal, never just accept your bank's first offer — always get competing quotes. Consider accelerated bi-weekly payments, which make one extra monthly payment per year and can cut 3-4 years off a 25-year amortisation"},
    "denmark": {"name":"Denmark","code":"denmark","iso":"DK","sym":"kr","cb":"Danmarks Nationalbank","deposit":"5% minimum","ltv":"up to 95% (80% covered bond + 15% bank loan)","govFirst":"First-time buyers benefit from mortgage interest tax deductibility at approximately 33% for amounts up to kr 50,000 per person. Some municipalities offer discounted building plots. Denmark's covered bond system provides some of the lowest fixed rates in Europe, giving first-time buyers access to affordable 30-year fixed-rate mortgages","savingsTips":"Denmark does not have a specific first-time buyer savings scheme like other countries. Focus on building a 5-10% cash deposit while minimising existing debts. Consider BSU (Boligopsparing for Ungdom) if still available — a tax-advantaged savings account for young buyers. Maintain a stable employment record","preApproval":"Contact a realkredit institute (Nykredit, Realkredit Danmark, or Totalkredit) and your bank for a combined financing plan. The realkredit provides up to 80% through covered bonds, and your bank provides the top-up loan. Get a tilsagn (commitment) before house hunting","mistakes":"Not understanding the two-layer financing structure (realkredit + bank loan at different rates), underestimating bidrag (administration fees) which are an ongoing percentage-based cost, rushing into the budrunde (bidding process) without financing in place, and not budgeting for the bank top-up loan which carries a higher rate than the covered bond portion","costs_breakdown":"Tinglysningsafgift (registration duty) of 0.6% of the purchase price plus kr 1,850 for property transfer. Mortgage registration of 1.45% of the loan amount plus kr 1,850. Lawyer fees of kr 10,000-25,000. Agent commission is paid by the seller. Tilstandsrapport (condition report) is typically provided by the seller. Ejerskifteforsikring (change-of-ownership insurance) of kr 5,000-20,000 is optional but recommended. Total buyer costs: 2-5%","costs_hidden":"Bidrag (administration fees) of 0.5-1.5% per year on the outstanding covered bond balance are an ongoing cost that significantly affects total mortgage cost over time. The bank top-up loan (for the 80-95% LTV portion) carries a much higher interest rate than the covered bond rate — budget accordingly. Ejendomsværdiskat (property value tax) is an annual cost based on the public valuation","mortgage_types":"Denmark's unique covered bond system creates distinctive mortgage types. **Fast rente (fixed rate)** locks your rate for 10, 15, 20, or 30 years through covered bonds — you can prepay by buying back your bonds at market price, which is a unique feature. **Rentetilpasningslån (adjustable rate)** resets every 1, 3, or 5 years based on new bond auctions — lower initial rate but renewal risk. **Capped variable** adds a maximum rate ceiling to the adjustable product, providing a safety net. The covered bond system means rates are set by the capital market, not bank margins, creating exceptional transparency","mortgage_compare":"Fixed rate gives 30 years of certainty at very competitive rates by European standards. Adjustable rates start 0.5-1.5% lower but carry renewal risk. The unique bond buyback feature means fixed-rate borrowers can profit if rates rise (bonds fall below par, letting you retire your mortgage at a discount). Capped variable offers a compromise — lower cost with a ceiling on risk","mortgage_tip":"Take advantage of the bond buyback mechanism — if rates rise significantly, you can retire your fixed-rate mortgage at a discount by buying back the underlying bonds at below par value. Always compare the total annual cost (ÅOP) rather than just the interest rate, as bidrag and fees vary between mortgage banks"},
    "france": {"name":"France","code":"france","iso":"FR","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","deposit":"10-20%","ltv":"typically 80-90% for first-time buyers","govFirst":"The Prêt à Taux Zéro (PTZ) is a zero-interest government loan covering up to 40% of the purchase price for first-time buyers of new-build or heavily renovated properties. Income limits apply by zone and household size. The PAS (Prêt d'Accession Sociale) offers subsidised rates for lower-income buyers. Action Logement provides employer-assisted loans at reduced rates for employees of companies with 10+ staff","savingsTips":"Open a PEL (Plan d'Épargne Logement) — a regulated savings account specifically for property purchase. After 4 years, you earn the right to a preferential mortgage rate. A CEL (Compte d'Épargne Logement) offers more flexibility with shorter lock-in. Both products demonstrate savings discipline to lenders","preApproval":"Use a courtier (mortgage broker) — services like Meilleurtaux, Cafpi, and Empruntis compare across all major banks for free or low cost. The courtier handles your dossier and negotiates on your behalf. Get an accord de principe (agreement in principle) before signing the compromis de vente","mistakes":"Not checking PTZ eligibility before committing to a property (the loan must be for a new build or qualifying renovation in an eligible zone), underestimating frais de notaire (7-8% for existing properties), exceeding the 35% taux d'endettement (debt-to-income) limit which is strictly enforced, and not using the mandatory 10-day reflection period on the mortgage offer to compare alternatives","costs_breakdown":"Frais de notaire dominate buying costs: 7-8% for existing properties (droits de mutation/registration taxes plus notary fees) or 2-3% for new builds (reduced tax rate). These include approximately 5.8% in taxes and 1-2% in notary fees and disbursements. Frais de dossier (bank processing fees) of €500-1,500. Frais de courtage (broker fees) of €1,000-2,000 if using a courtier. Assurance emprunteur (mandatory borrower's insurance) of 0.2-0.5% of the loan annually. Frais de garantie (mortgage guarantee via Crédit Logement or hypothèque) of 1-2%","costs_hidden":"Assurance emprunteur (borrower's insurance covering death, disability, and potentially job loss) is mandatory and can add 0.2-0.5% to your effective rate — over a 20-year loan this can total €15,000-30,000. Under the Lemoine law, you can switch provider at any time for a cheaper alternative. Charges de copropriété (condominium fees) for apartments and taxe foncière (annual property tax) are ongoing costs that lenders factor into affordability","mortgage_types":"France is distinctive for offering long-term fully fixed rates. **Taux fixe (fixed rate)** locks your rate for 15, 20, or 25 years — the dominant product, chosen by the vast majority of French borrowers. This gives extraordinary payment certainty. **Taux variable (variable rate)** adjusts with the Euribor — less common in France (under 10% of market) and carries rate risk. **Taux variable capé (capped variable)** has a maximum rate ceiling (typically +1% or +2% above the initial rate) providing a safety net. All French mortgages are amortising — you repay principal and interest monthly. Interest-only is available only for investment purposes","mortgage_compare":"Fixed rate is the default choice in France — with 20-25 year fully fixed rates available at competitive levels, the certainty is hard to beat. Variable rates save money when Euribor is low but carry risk. Capped variable offers a compromise but the cap premium can offset savings. French rates are generally among the lowest in Europe for fixed-rate products, making the fixed-rate choice even more compelling","mortgage_tip":"Use a courtier (broker) — they negotiate across multiple banks and often secure rates 0.1-0.3% below what you would get walking into a branch. The assurance emprunteur is a major hidden cost — use délégation d'assurance to get external insurance from day one rather than the bank's more expensive group contract. Under the Lemoine law, you can switch insurer at any time"},
    "germany": {"name":"Germany","code":"germany","iso":"DE","sym":"€","cb":"European Central Bank","deposit":"20-30%","ltv":"typically 70-80%","govFirst":"KfW Bank offers subsidised loans for energy-efficient new builds through KfW 261, with below-market rates and repayment bonuses. Some Bundesländer (federal states) have their own programmes — Bavaria's BayernLabo and NRW's NRW.BANK offer targeted support. Wohn-Riester allows using state-subsidised retirement savings toward mortgage repayments. Direct buyer subsidies are limited at the federal level since the end of Baukindergeld","savingsTips":"Build a substantial deposit of 20-30% plus enough for Kaufnebenkosten (transaction costs of 10-15%). Consider a Bausparvertrag (building savings contract) — a traditional German product combining savings with a future loan at a pre-agreed rate. Maintain a clean SCHUFA record, as any negative entries significantly impact mortgage approval","preApproval":"Use a mortgage broker (Vermittler) like Interhyp or Dr. Klein — they compare over 400 lenders for free. Get a Finanzierungszusage (financing commitment) before starting your property search. Ensure your SCHUFA score is checked and clean before applying","mistakes":"Underestimating Kaufnebenkosten (10-15% of purchase price that must come from savings), not checking your SCHUFA score early (negative entries take years to clear), choosing too short a Zinsbindung (fixed-rate period) to save money without understanding the refinancing risk, and not shopping around with a Vermittler (broker) — individual bank offers vary significantly","costs_breakdown":"Grunderwerbsteuer (real estate transfer tax) is the largest cost: 3.5% in Bavaria and Saxony up to 6.5% in Brandenburg, NRW, and Schleswig-Holstein. Notar und Grundbuchkosten (notary and land registry fees) of approximately 1.5-2%. Maklergebühr (agent commission) of 3-6% is now split equally between buyer and seller (since December 2020 reform). Total Kaufnebenkosten: 10-15% of the purchase price, all of which must come from your own savings","costs_hidden":"The full Kaufnebenkosten (10-15%) must be paid from savings — banks do not lend for these costs. Agent commission reform means buyers pay 50% of the Maklergebühr, typically 3-3.5% including VAT. Grundsteuer (annual property tax) is an ongoing cost that was reformed in 2025 and varies significantly by municipality. Hausgeld (management fees for apartments) covers maintenance, insurance, and building reserves — typically €200-500/month","mortgage_types":"Germany's Zinsbindung system is unlike most countries. **Annuitätendarlehen (annuity loan)** is the standard product — fixed monthly payments with a Zinsbindung (fixed-rate period) of 5, 10, 15, or 20 years. When the Zinsbindung expires, you refinance (Anschlussfinanzierung) at current rates. **Volltilger (full repayment loan)** repays entirely within the fixed period — higher payments but no refinancing risk. **KfW-Darlehen** are subsidised government loans for energy-efficient properties at below-market rates. **Bauspardarlehen** combines a savings phase with a guaranteed future loan rate. Under §489 BGB, you can exit any fixed-rate loan after 10 years with 6 months' notice, regardless of the original Zinsbindung","mortgage_compare":"A 10-year Zinsbindung is the most popular choice — balancing rate security with cost. Longer periods (15-20 years) cost 0.3-0.5% more per year but eliminate refinancing risk for longer. A Volltilger (full repayment within the fixed period) gets a rate discount but requires higher monthly payments. The §489 BGB right to exit after 10 years is a powerful safety valve for borrowers who chose a long Zinsbindung","mortgage_tip":"Use a Vermittler (broker) like Interhyp or Dr. Klein — they compare 400+ lenders for free. Choose a Tilgung (repayment rate) of at least 2-3% to build equity faster. Plan your Anschlussfinanzierung 12-36 months before your Zinsbindung expires using a Forward-Darlehen to lock in rates early"},
}

